[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says "one issue, one fix queued". The desk's own code and notes say
otherwise: the reports merged into it come from three different problems, and the queued parser fix
covers only the first.

<Callout kind="warn">
Shipping the parser fix will not close #4471. Reports from booking-v3 forms and the one-day moves
will keep coming.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemSplit ticket={4471} {issues} />

The table is built from `reports-4471.json` at render time. The export repeats some reports across
pages, so rows are counted once per id. Counts follow the desk's own view: each report sits under its
cause by tenant, so the `one-day` reports are also counted inside `parser`.

## The three problems

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads dates month first, and every tenant on file writes the day
  first. Day and month swap: "Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July."
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first
  tenants type what the hint says, and the form reads it the tenant's way. Fixing the parser does not
  touch this.
- **`one-day`**: the date moves by one day, not by a swap. "Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday",
  and one meeting landed on Tuesday in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto. That points to time zones. The
  desk already listed these reports in `desk-notes.json`; the #4471 page still files them under
  `parser` with a footnote.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line, and these reports
share a handful of subjects. A subject says what the reporter saw, not what broke. Intake tags follow
the reporter's browser, not the tenant, so they disagree with the cause too.

## What to do

- Keep #4471 for `parser`, and track `hint` and `one-day` as their own tickets.
- When a big ticket gets a fix queued, check whether every report in it would be closed by that fix.

